However, the marginal cost of buying the $k$-th unit of product $i$ is not constant.
The cost to go from $k-1$ units to $k$ units is $k^2 P_i - (k-1)^2 P_i = (k^2 - (k^2 - 2k + 1)) P_i = (2k - 1) P_i$.
So the marginal cost of the $k$-th unit is $(2k - 1) P_i$.
Notice that the marginal cost increases as $k$ increases. This means the cost function is convex.
To maximize the total units, we should greedily pick the cheapest available "next unit".
The "next unit" for product $i$ currently having $x_i$ units costs $(2(x_i+1) - 1) P_i = (2x_i + 1) P_i$.
Initially, all $x_i = 0$, so the cost of the first unit of product $i$ is $P_i$.
The cost of the second unit is $3 P_i$, the third is $5 P_i$, and so on.
The sequence of marginal costs for product $i$ is $P_i, 3P_i, 5P_i, \dots, (2k-1)P_i, \dots$.
We want to select a set of units such that the sum of their marginal costs is $\le M$ and the count is maximized.
